  <h3>Layer 0 &mdash; Invariants</h3>
  <p>
    The 8+1 hard rules: files-only state, append-only spine, no hosted backend, no broad new deps, no provider SDKs in app code, no token export, brand boundary, lane ownership, and the auto-trigger gauntlet. These are not policy layered on top of features &mdash; they are the things every feature has to be compatible with in order to exist. Doctor-enforced on every install and upgrade.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 1 &mdash; Operator surface</h3>
  <p>
    The operator types a slash command or a plain sentence; the verbose CLI stays for scripting. No one memorizes <code>maddu cmd subcmd --flag value</code>. There are 40 slash on-ramps installed per repo. The bar is that a brand-new user ships a slice without opening the docs.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 2 &mdash; Agent runtime</h3>
  <p>
    Any agent CLI plugs in &mdash; claude-code, codex, gemini, and future runtimes. The coordinator that fans work across phases is a Máddu primitive, not a Claude Code or Codex feature. That is what lets orchestration survive vendor pivots.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 3 &mdash; Execution topology</h3>
  <p>
    Three default pipelines, the coordinator primitive, the team verb, and ad-hoc <code>/maddu-autopilot</code> for one-offs. Rule of thumb: prefer a pipeline; ad-hoc is reserved for genuinely one-off changes.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 4 &mdash; Substrate loop</h3>
  <p>
    The one mental model: register, claim, slice, stop, release, close. Every transition is exactly one event. Pipelines, coordinator, and autopilot all reduce to this loop at higher altitudes, which is what makes replay deterministic.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 5 &mdash; Lanes</h3>
  <p>
    Concurrency without locks. Claim a lane before editing its file area; no two agents hold the same lane. Cross-lane work goes through a mailbox NDJSON, never shared mutation. Force-claims leave a contested-claim audit. This is Rule #8 made operable.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 6 &mdash; The gauntlet</h3>
  <p>
    Rule #9 made operable. Read-only actions pass through and are still logged. State-mutating actions must be allowlisted, must respect cooldowns, must satisfy the governance tier, and must emit a <code>TRIGGER_FIRED</code> event with provenance. It is the brake on confidently-wrong propagation.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 7 &mdash; Worker isolation</h3>
  <p>
    Provider calls live here and nowhere else. Subprocess workers spawn per slice with a default-deny env allowlist. Credentials are injected at spawn and never written to the spine. Tool argv passes a secret scanner before logging, and token usage is emitted as events. The framework itself never imports a provider SDK.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 8 &mdash; The spine</h3>
  <p>
    The single source of truth: append-only NDJSON under <code>.maddu/events/</code>, with {EVENT_TYPES} event types covering session lifecycle, lane operations, slices, approvals, triggers, token usage, trust audits, MCP provenance, secret-scan blocks, governance changes, and audit reports. A forward <code>prev_hash</code> hash-chain makes interior tampering evident. The spine is verified, never auto-repaired.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 9 &mdash; Projections</h3>
  <p>
    Plans, kanban, receipts, cost, skills, trust ledger, governance state &mdash; all JSON projections under <code>.maddu/state/</code>. All are rebuildable from the spine, none are authoritative, and gates enforce their determinism.
  </p>

  <div class="dx-note">
    <span class="dx-note-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◆</span>
    <p><strong>Projections rebuild from the spine and are never authoritative.</strong> If a projection and the spine ever disagree, the spine wins &mdash; the projection is discarded and rebuilt from events. State is never repaired in place.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Layer 10 &mdash; Security &amp; supply chain</h3>
  <p>
    Structural, not advisory. <code>maddu trust</code> pins dependencies with SHA256, MCP servers come from an allowlisted gallery, skill imports require the <code>--trust</code> flag, and sources and approvals gate provenance. The threat-model doc is the operator's security manual.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 11 &mdash; Quality &amp; invariant enforcement</h3>
  <p>
    Enforcement, not aspiration:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>{DOCTOR_GATES} doctor gates on every install and upgrade</li>
    <li>16-check charter-drift audit, now including architecture-drift</li>
    <li>15-scenario stress harness and a four-version upgrade matrix</li>
    <li>Framework-source self-test versus host-project test</li>
    <li>Layout-refusal at every release boundary; review, test, lint, and format at slice level</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Layer 12 &mdash; Memory &amp; accounting</h3>
  <p>
    Work outlives the agent. Hindsight skills auto-curate from slice-stops, threshold-gated and with cooldowns. <code>maddu learn</code> mines failed&rarr;succeeded tool-call pairs into durable corrections; <code>maddu blueprint</code> exports a portable, variable-driven project handoff; <code>maddu debt</code> ledgers the deliberate shortcuts. The receipt log accounts for spend, operator memory persists decisions and their rationale, and replay determinism means past state is reconstructible.
  </p>

  <h3>Layer 13 &mdash; Cockpit</h3>
  <p>
    Observe and replay, never command. A static local page that runs in any browser over long-poll &mdash; no websocket, no cloud. Its 38+ routes are grouped by intent (Decide, Operate, Verify, Connect, Reference), with an entity-drawer primitive for click-through detail.
  </p>

  <h2>How state flows</h2>
  <p>
    State moves in one direction. A transition in the substrate loop &mdash; or any gate in the gauntlet &mdash; appends exactly one event to the spine. Nothing mutates an earlier record; the log only grows.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Vertical is causal flow: operator &rarr; runtime &rarr; execution &rarr; substrate &rarr; lanes &rarr; gauntlet &rarr; worker &rarr; spine.</li>
    <li>The side channels &mdash; security, quality, memory &mdash; bind across layers rather than sitting in the vertical stack.</li>
    <li>Every write lands on the append-only spine, so any run replays end to end.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="dx-note">
    <span class="dx-note-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◆</span>
    <p><strong>The posture is deliberate, not missing.</strong> No scheduler thread (the spine is the write-ahead log), no worker queue (lane claims are the lock), no mutex layer, no websocket, no SaaS or telemetry, no provider SDKs in app code, no auto-repair, and no cross-machine token sync.</p>
  </div>

